1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:05,490
Slide 6 reveals the technical dilemma: BYPASSING THE BROWSER SANDBOX.

2
00:00:05,890 --> 00:00:11,103
For twenty years, web browsers were engineered as airtight security prisons.

3
00:00:11,103 --> 00:00:15,150
Why did Google break out of their own Chrome prison, James?

4
00:00:15,550 --> 00:00:19,214
Because the browser sandbox has strict security handcuffs!

5
00:00:19,214 --> 00:00:31,220
Look at the left card: a webpage inside Chrome cannot touch your local `C:\` drive, cannot capture your desktop screen without annoying popups, and cannot listen for global keyboard hotkeys!

6
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:32,820
Exactly!

7
00:00:32,820 --> 00:00:44,820
If you want an AI that can organize your local Downloads folder, inspect your VS Code editor, and summarize a PDF sitting on your desktop, you MUST have native OS shell privileges on the right card!

8
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:48,867
But with great OS privilege comes massive security danger!

9
00:00:48,867 --> 00:00:56,980
If a malicious prompt injection compromises a desktop agent, it has access to your entire local hard drive and all your SSH keys!

10
00:00:57,380 --> 00:01:03,400
That's why desktop agents require ten times more security hardening than simple web chatbots!

11
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:10,060
That is why local-first architecture requires strict tenant boundaries and cryptographic audits.

